# tools/r/room_bound_witness.rish -- no room grows past what a reader can open.
#
# WHY THIS RUNG. `session-logs/` held 1,492 dated files at room level on `20260821`, and GitHub's
# web listing stops at 1,000 entries, so the room was genuinely unopenable in a browser. The fold
# to `date/YYYYMMDD/` fixed it. This rung is what keeps it fixed -- because at roughly a hundred
# and eight logs a day, an emptied room refills past a thousand in nine days, and a one-time sweep
# would have bought nine days. The bound is the root fix; the sweep was only the symptom.
#
# WHAT IS PROVEN HERE.
#
#   Every room is discovered rather than listed. `tools/fixtures/room_bound_scan.sh` walks every
#   top-level directory holding a dated file at its own level, so a room created tomorrow appears
#   in the report without anyone remembering to add it.
#
#   The enforced roster holds, room by room. Five rooms earned enforcement by folding --
#   `session-logs`, then `counsel`, `active-designing`, `expanding-prompts`, and `waymarks` --
#   `active-development` was born enforced on `20260821.174047`, having no backlog to grandfather,
#   and `tools` joined on `20260823.144100` the same way the first five did, by folding.
#   Each is asserted by name, because a roster checked only in aggregate can lose a room
#   without anyone noticing. Three of the seven hold ZERO flat files now, so a discovery-only report
#   dropped them entirely; an enforced room is reported whether or not it holds anything. Two hundred fifty-six rather than a thousand, because a bound
#   placed at the cliff fails on the day it matters.
#
#   TWO COUNTING RULES, and the second exists because the first was blind. The six day-folded
#   rooms are counted by dated basename, which is what their fold moves. `tools/` folds by first
#   sprig letter and not one of its 1,917 entries carried a stamp, so the dated count read ZERO for
#   that room for its whole life while the room stood 7.4x over bound with every guard green. It is
#   counted by every flat entry instead, symlinks included, and the scan prints `counts=all` beside
#   it so a reader knows which rule answered.
#
#   Every other room is reported and books nothing. None stands over the bound today, and
#   `external-research` at 160 is the next likely to cross -- it will be reported for a while
#   before anyone has to act. A ratchet turns on touch; a sweep nobody asked for is not a fix.
#
#   Two RED paths are proven by construction, since the gate can fail in two directions. A
#   throwaway room built over a deliberately tiny bound must be refused BY NAME. And a tree
#   holding none of the enforced rooms must also be refused, because a room that vanishes from a
#   meter is not a room that passed it -- it is a room nobody is watching.
#
# WHAT IS NOT PROVEN. This counts files; it does not judge whether a room's contents belong
# together. A room could satisfy the bound and still be badly organised, and no integer will
# notice. Nesting IS read now -- the undated sweep asks git for every directory -- yet what the
# sweep reports it does not gate, and for a day shelf it cannot: the mark law provides no shape
# below `date/YYYYMMDD/`, so `terminal_shelf=` names a standing fact rather than a task. Whether
# such a shelf should ever fold -- by hour, `date/YYYYMMDD/HH/`, which is equally pure since the
# hour is characters 10 and 11 of the basename -- is a law change and Keaton's word (REDS %315).
#
# Chapter: the Standfast restrategizing of `20260821`, arc one -- the mark and the path.
# Kin: .claude/rules/stamp-and-name.md (the fold's law) - tools/d/dated_path_witness.rish (the
# resolver that makes a fold safe) - tools/s/session_logs_archive.rish (the fold itself).
#
# Run from the repository root:
#   rishi/bin/rishi run tools/r/room_bound_witness.rish

say "room-bound: Language EN -- a room that outgrows a reader stops being a room and starts being a pile. This holds every enforced room at a size a person can open, and reports the rest honestly rather than sweeping them unasked."
say "room-bound: Style Radiant -- three claims. Rooms are discovered rather than listed. All eight enforced rooms are named and stand under 256, six counted by dated basename and two -- tools and glow/gen -- by every flat entry. And the gate is proven to close in both directions: on a room over its bound, and on an enforced room that has gone missing."
say "room-bound: Lens TAME -- the bound is named at 256 rather than at GitHub's 1,000, because a maximum set at the cliff fails on the day it matters; every count below is the scan's own output rather than this file's memory."

let scan = "tools/fixtures/room_bound_scan.sh"

let roster = run ["sh" scan]
# The message names BOTH causes, because it fired for the second one on a real clone and sent a
# reader looking for a room to fold when the room was simply absent (REDS %120).
assert roster.ok else "room-bound: an ENFORCED room either stands OVER the bound -- fold it to date/YYYYMMDD/ with tools/s/session_logs_archive.rish -- or is MISSING from this tree entirely, which on a fresh clone means it never entered the .gitignore allow-list. Read the roster lines above: verdict=over is the first, verdict=missing is the second."
say roster.out
assert roster.out contains "bound=256" else "room-bound: the seated bound is 256 -- a change to it is a seated decision, not a default"
assert roster.out contains "enforced_over=0" else "room-bound: every enforced room must stand at or under the bound"
assert roster.out contains "verdict=ok" else "room-bound: the roster must balance before it is believed"
# Every enforced room is named, one assert apiece. A roster checked only in aggregate can lose a
# room without anyone noticing -- `counsel` and `expanding-prompts` reached zero flat files on the
# lap they folded and dropped straight off a discovery-only report.
assert roster.out contains "room=session-logs flat=" else "room-bound: session-logs must appear in the roster -- a room that vanishes from a meter is not a room that passed it"
assert roster.out contains "room=counsel flat=" else "room-bound: counsel is enforced and must be reported even at zero flat files"
assert roster.out contains "room=active-designing flat=" else "room-bound: active-designing is enforced and must be reported"
assert roster.out contains "room=active-development flat=" else "room-bound: active-development is enforced from birth and must be reported even while empty"
assert roster.out contains "room=expanding-prompts flat=" else "room-bound: expanding-prompts is enforced and must be reported even at zero flat files"
assert roster.out contains "room=waymarks flat=" else "room-bound: waymarks is enforced and must be reported"
# The two letter-folded rooms, by name as well. The header above claims every enforced room is
# asserted here, and until `20260828` these two were the exception -- which is the wrong pair to
# leave unnamed, since counting by every flat entry is the rule `tools/` needed after reading
# zero for its whole life at 7.4x the bound.
assert roster.out contains "room=tools flat=" else "room-bound: tools is enforced by every flat entry and must be reported by name"
assert roster.out contains "room=glow/gen flat=" else "room-bound: glow/gen joined the enforced roster on 20260828 and must be reported by name"
# The sweep prints two counters and they must both appear, because a counter that vanishes
# reads exactly like a counter at zero.
assert roster.out contains "undated_over=" else "room-bound: the undated sweep must report how many rooms stand over the bound with a fold ahead of them"
assert roster.out contains "terminal_shelves=" else "room-bound: the undated sweep must report how many day shelves stand over the bound with no fold the mark law provides"
assert roster.out contains "roster=enforce" else "room-bound: at least one room must be enforced, or the gate guards nothing"

# The RED path, built rather than trusted. A room of three dated files under a bound of two must
# be refused, and refused by name, so the gate is known to close.
let control = run ["sh" "tools/fixtures/room_bound_control.sh"]
assert control.ok else "room-bound: the two-phase control must complete"
say control.out
assert control.out contains "over_named_by_room=yes" else "room-bound: an over-bound room must be refused by name rather than by a bare count"
assert control.out contains "over_refused=yes" else "room-bound: a room over its bound must exit non-zero"
# This number is the roster size on purpose. It moved 5 -> 6 when active-development opened, and
# the assert caught it -- which is the point: a room joining or leaving the roster is a seated
# decision, so it should have to be written down here rather than sliding in unremarked. It caught
# the same thing again on `20260828`: `glow/gen` joined ENFORCE_ALL in commit 9b58744ef once the
# compiler's two import-path sites learned the letter room, taking the roster 7 -> 8 while this
# line stayed at 7 -- so the gate that watches every room refused on its own roster until the
# number was turned by hand. That is the assert doing its job, and it is also the standing cost of
# the design: the roster lives in the scan and its size lives here, so the two move in one lap or
# the gate reds (REDS %315).
assert control.out contains "enforced_rooms_named_absent=8" else "room-bound: every enforced room absent from a tree must be named absent, not passed over -- and this count is the roster size, so a change here means a room joined or left"
assert control.out contains "all_counted_room_named=yes" else "room-bound: a letter-folded room over bound must red on its own counting rule and say which rule it used"
assert control.out contains "missing_refused=yes" else "room-bound: an enforced room missing from the tree must refuse -- a room nobody can find has not passed"
# PHASE THREE, the undated sweep. Seven readings, and each is proven from both sides inside the
# control itself: the two shapes planted in a real git pen, then removed, so a line that always
# printed would fail the second half. Planting a regression in the scan flips three of the
# seven and the control refuses, which is how this was checked rather than assumed.
assert control.out contains "day_shelf_named_terminal=yes" else "room-bound: a day shelf over the bound must be named terminal -- the mark law folds WHEN-rooms to date/YYYYMMDD/ and there is no shape below it"
assert control.out contains "day_shelf_kept_off_fold_list=yes" else "room-bound: a day shelf must NOT also print as a room with a fold ahead of it, or the meter books work the law cannot perform"
assert control.out contains "foldable_room_named=yes" else "room-bound: an ordinary nested room over the bound must still be reported for its letter fold"
assert control.out contains "foldable_room_kept_off_shelf_list=yes" else "room-bound: a room that folds by letter must not be excused as a terminal shelf"
assert control.out contains "sweep_counts_split=yes" else "room-bound: the two shapes must be counted apart, or the split is cosmetic"
assert control.out contains "sweep_moved_no_gate=yes" else "room-bound: the sweep reports and gates nothing -- 256 sits below the 1,000-entry cliff on purpose, and a shelf bound is a seated law rather than a meter's to make"
assert control.out contains "both_readings_silent_when_removed=yes" else "room-bound: both readings must fall silent once their plants are gone -- a reading proven only in the firing direction cannot be told from a line that always prints"
assert control.out contains "control_verdict=ok" else "room-bound: the control must agree with itself before it is believed"
say "RED paths GREEN -- a room over its bound refuses by name, and an enforced room absent from the tree refuses too"

say "GREEN: room-bound -- rooms discovered rather than listed, all eight enforced rooms named and under 256 (six by dated basename, tools and glow/gen by every flat entry), a day shelf over the bound told apart from a room with a fold ahead of it, the rest reported and booking nothing, and the gate proven to close in three directions: on a dated room over its bound, on a letter-folded room over its bound, and on an enforced room gone missing."
